<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-theoretical-probability" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Theoretical Probability</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>3.0 Statistics and Probability</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>3.2 Probability II</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title>
                    <p>Determine theoretical probability in different situations</p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>11</p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>40 minutes</p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>12 minutes</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To help learners discover that listing all outcomes of a multi-item draw quickly becomes impractical, motivating the use of combinations to count sample spaces.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Let groups struggle with listing pairs for part (a) before moving on. Once they have the list, ask: <q>What if there were 20 buttons — would you still want to list every pair?</q> Use their discomfort to motivate part (d).
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="act-buttons-in-a-bag">
        <title>Buttons in a Bag</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Amina has a bag containing 3 red buttons and 2 blue buttons. She picks 2 buttons at random without looking.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Label the red buttons <m>R_1, R_2, R_3</m> and the blue buttons <m>B_1, B_2</m>. List every possible pair of buttons Amina could draw.
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                There are 10 pairs:
                                <me>R_1R_2,\quad R_1R_3,\quad R_2R_3,\quad R_1B_1,\quad R_1B_2,\quad R_2B_1,\quad R_2B_2,\quad R_3B_1,\quad R_3B_2,\quad B_1B_2.</me>
                            </p>
                            <p>
                                Encourage groups to work systematically: fix one button and list all its possible partners before moving to the next.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            How many of those pairs consist of 2 red buttons? Hence find <m>P(\text{both red})</m>.
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                Three pairs are all-red: <m>R_1R_2,\ R_1R_3,\ R_2R_3</m>.
                                So <m>P(\text{both red}) = \dfrac{3}{10}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Suppose instead the bag had 8 red buttons and 5 blue buttons. How many pairs would there be in total? Would listing them all be practical?
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                There are 13 buttons in total. At this point learners may not yet know how to compute the number of pairs — let them attempt a count or estimate, and leave the precise answer for part (d).
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            In the previous chapter you learned that the number of ways to choose <m>r</m> items from <m>n</m> is
                            <me>{}^{n}C_{r} = \frac{n!}{r!\,(n-r)!}.</me>
                            Use <m>{}^{5}C_{2}</m> to check your answer to part (a). Then use <m>{}^{13}C_{2}</m> to answer part (c).
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                <m>{}^{5}C_{2} = \dfrac{5!}{2!\,3!} = 10</m>. This matches the list — a good sign that the formula works.
                            </p>
                            <p>
                                <m>{}^{13}C_{2} = \dfrac{13!}{2!\,11!} = 78</m>. Listing 78 pairs by hand would be very tedious; the formula is essential for larger problems.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>12 minutes</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To formalise the connection between the activity and the probability formula, showing how combinations count both the sample space and the favourable event.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Return to the activity. The sample space in part (a) had <m>{}^{5}C_{2} = 10</m> equally-likely outcomes. The event <q>both red</q> had <m>{}^{3}C_{2} = 3</m> outcomes. Write
                    <me>P(E) = \frac{n(E)}{n(S)} = \frac{{}^{3}C_{2}}{{}^{5}C_{2}} = \frac{3}{10}</me>
                    on the board and confirm it matches the listed answer.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Then show a two-group example on the same bag: the event <q>exactly 1 blue</q> means choosing 1 blue from 2 <em>and</em> 1 red from 3, so <m>n(E) = {}^{2}C_{1} \times {}^{3}C_{1} = 6</m>. The multiplication comes from the counting principle learners met in the combinatorics chapter.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <insight>
        <title>Key Ideas</title>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <term>Theoretical probability</term>: when all outcomes are equally likely,
                        <me>P(E) = \frac{n(E)}{n(S)},</me>
                        where <m>n(E)</m> is the number of outcomes in event <m>E</m> and <m>n(S)</m> is the total number of outcomes in the sample space <m>S</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>P(E)</m> is always between 0 and 1. An impossible event has probability 0; a certain event has probability 1.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        When <m>r</m> items are drawn simultaneously from a group of <m>n</m>, the sample space has
                        <me>n(S) = {}^{n}C_{r}</me>
                        equally-likely outcomes.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        When an event requires selecting from <em>two groups</em> — say <m>j</m> items from group A (of size <m>a</m>) and <m>k</m> items from group B (of size <m>b</m>) — multiply the combinations:
                        <me>n(E) = {}^{a}C_{j} \times {}^{b}C_{k}.</me>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        For <q>at least one</q> problems, the <term>complement rule</term> is often easier:
                        <me>P(\text{at least one}) = 1 - P(\text{none}).</me>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </insight>

    <p>
        In Grade 10 you learned that <m>P(E) = \dfrac{\text{number of favourable outcomes}}{\text{total number of outcomes}}</m> for equally-likely outcomes, and practised this by listing outcomes for single draws from bags, decks of cards, and dice. In this section we extend that idea: when we draw <em>more than one item at a time</em>, we use combinations to count the outcomes instead of listing them.
    </p>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example>
        <title>Single Group</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A bag contains 5 red marbles and 3 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn at random. Find:
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p><m>P(\text{both red})</m></p></li>
                    <li><p><m>P(\text{both blue})</m></p></li>
                    <li><p><m>P(\text{one red and one blue})</m></p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                There are 8 marbles in total and we draw 2, so the sample space has
                <me>n(S) = {}^{8}C_{2} = \frac{8!}{2!\,6!} = 28 \text{ outcomes.}</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Choose 2 red from the 5 red marbles:
                            <me>n(E) = {}^{5}C_{2} = 10, \qquad P(\text{both red}) = \frac{10}{28} = \frac{5}{14}.</me>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Choose 2 blue from the 3 blue marbles:
                            <me>n(E) = {}^{3}C_{2} = 3, \qquad P(\text{both blue}) = \frac{3}{28}.</me>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Choose 1 red from 5 <em>and</em> 1 blue from 3:
                            <me>n(E) = {}^{5}C_{1} \times {}^{3}C_{1} = 5 \times 3 = 15, \qquad P(\text{one each}) = \frac{15}{28}.</me>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
            <p>
                Check: <m>10 + 3 + 15 = 28 = n(S)</m>. The three events cover every possible outcome, so their probabilities must sum to 1: <m>\dfrac{10 + 3 + 15}{28} = 1</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Selecting from Two Groups</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A school committee of 3 is chosen at random from 4 boys and 6 girls. Find the probability that the committee contains exactly 2 girls.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The committee is chosen from 10 students in total:
                <me>n(S) = {}^{10}C_{3} = \frac{10!}{3!\,7!} = 120.</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                <q>Exactly 2 girls</q> means 2 chosen from the 6 girls and 1 chosen from the 4 boys:
                <me>n(E) = {}^{6}C_{2} \times {}^{4}C_{1} = 15 \times 4 = 60.</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Therefore
                <me>P(\text{exactly 2 girls}) = \frac{60}{120} = \frac{1}{2}.</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Using the Complement Rule</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                In a class of 12 students (7 boys and 5 girls), 4 are selected at random to represent the class at a sports event. Find the probability that the group includes at least one girl.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <me>n(S) = {}^{12}C_{4} = \frac{12!}{4!\,8!} = 495.</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                The complement of <q>at least one girl</q> is <q>no girls at all</q>, i.e. all 4 are boys:
                <me>n(\text{all boys}) = {}^{7}C_{4} = 35, \qquad P(\text{all boys}) = \frac{35}{495} = \frac{7}{99}.</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                By the complement rule:
                <me>P(\text{at least one girl}) = 1 - \frac{7}{99} = \frac{92}{99}.</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Seats in a Matatu</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A matatu has 10 seats: 3 window seats and 7 aisle seats. Four passengers board and are each assigned a seat at random. Find the probability that exactly 2 of them sit at window seats.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Any 4 of the 10 seats are occupied:
                <me>n(S) = {}^{10}C_{4} = \frac{10!}{4!\,6!} = 210.</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                <q>Exactly 2 window seats</q> means 2 chosen from the 3 window seats and 2 chosen from the 7 aisle seats:
                <me>n(E) = {}^{3}C_{2} \times {}^{7}C_{2} = 3 \times 21 = 63.</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Therefore
                <me>P(\text{exactly 2 window seats}) = \frac{63}{210} = \frac{3}{10}.</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A bag contains 4 green counters and 6 yellow counters. One counter is drawn at random. Find the probability that it is green.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>P(\text{green}) = \dfrac{4}{10} = \dfrac{2}{5}</m>.
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A box holds 6 pens: 4 black and 2 red. Three pens are drawn at random. Find the probability that all three pens are black.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>n(S) = {}^{6}C_{3} = 20</m>. The number of all-black selections is <m>{}^{4}C_{3} = 4</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <m>P(\text{all black}) = \dfrac{4}{20} = \dfrac{1}{5}</m>.
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A committee of 4 students is chosen at random from a group of 5 Science students and 4 Arts students. Find:
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>The probability that all 4 are Science students.</p></li>
                        <li><p>The probability that exactly 3 are Science students and 1 is an Arts student.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>n(S) = {}^{9}C_{4} = 126</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>{}^{5}C_{4} = 5</m>, so <m>P = \dfrac{5}{126}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>{}^{5}C_{3} \times {}^{4}C_{1} = 10 \times 4 = 40</m>, so <m>P = \dfrac{40}{126} = \dfrac{20}{63}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    From a group of 8 friends (5 boys and 3 girls), 3 are chosen at random to represent their school. Find the probability that at least one of them is a girl.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>n(S) = {}^{8}C_{3} = 56</m>. The complement (all boys) gives <m>{}^{5}C_{3} = 10</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <m>P(\text{at least one girl}) = 1 - \dfrac{10}{56} = \dfrac{46}{56} = \dfrac{23}{28}</m>.
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A hand of 3 cards is dealt at random from a set of 10 cards numbered 1 through 10.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Find the total number of possible hands.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find the probability that all three cards show even numbers.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find the probability that the hand contains exactly one odd-numbered card.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    The set contains 5 even numbers (2, 4, 6, 8, 10) and 5 odd numbers (1, 3, 5, 7, 9).
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>{}^{10}C_{3} = 120</m> possible hands.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                All even: <m>{}^{5}C_{3} = 10</m>, so <m>P = \dfrac{10}{120} = \dfrac{1}{12}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Exactly 1 odd and 2 even: <m>{}^{5}C_{1} \times {}^{5}C_{2} = 5 \times 10 = 50</m>, so <m>P = \dfrac{50}{120} = \dfrac{5}{12}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>

</section>
